## 🎯 How they differ — pros / cons depending on your goals

* **Full networks with feeds (Awin, Webgains, TradeTracker, etc.)** — good if you want to build a “real” affiliate site tied to legit retailers/brands from day one. You get “official” feeds with images, pricing, and up-to-date product info.
* **Feed-aggregators / services (Feeds4.com, FMTC Datafeed)** — best if you want **volume and variety** (many merchants, many categories) without joining each network individually. More “data-pool” style: ideal for large catalogues, price comparison sites, or multi-niche content.
* **Site-integration tools/plugins (Datafeedr, WooCommerce importers, etc.)** — extremely useful if your site uses a CMS (e.g. WordPress). They simplify the “import → display → update” pipeline: you don’t need to manually write code or build custom parsers.

---

## ⚠️ What to check / be cautious about

* **Licensing & compliance** — even if you get images and descriptions, you must ensure the feed provider (or merchant) allows you to display them publicly. Some networks limit how and where images or brand assets can be used.
* **Feed freshness / accuracy** — products go out of stock, prices change, items get discontinued — using a feed means you need a way to continuously update. Aggregators often help with that, but cheaper/older feeds may become stale.
* **Quality & uniqueness** — if you just dump raw feed content with no added value (reviews, commentary, curation), your site may look generic or “thin content” — bad for SEO and user trust. It’s best to build editorial/contextual content *around* feed items.
* **Costs / subscription fees** — some of these services aren’t free (especially feed-aggregators or enterprise-grade providers). Factor that into your budget.

